/-!
# Adjoint and coadjoint orbits of the Heisenberg group

The Heisenberg group `HeisenbergGroup R` of triples `(x, y, z)` is the group of unipotent upper
triangular matrices `g = !![1, x, z; 0, 1, y; 0, 0, 1]` (`TauCeti.HeisenbergGroup.toMatrix`), and
its Lie algebra is the Heisenberg Lie algebra `TauCeti.heisenberg R` of strictly upper triangular
`3 × 3` matrices, with basis `X = E₀₁`, `Y = E₁₂`, `Z = E₀₂` and only bracket `⁅X, Y⁆ = Z`. This
file computes the orbits of the adjoint and coadjoint actions of the group, and the
Kirillov–Kostant–Souriau form of the coadjoint orbits, over an arbitrary commutative ring `R`.

The adjoint action is conjugation, `Ad_g A = g A g⁻¹`
(`TauCeti.HeisenbergGroup.adjointRepresentation`), and the coadjoint action is its dual,
`(Ad*_g ν)(A) = ν (Ad_(g⁻¹) A)` (`TauCeti.HeisenbergGroup.coadjointRepresentation`), as for the
coadjoint representation of a Lie group. Write `N = g - 1` for the nilpotent part of `g`
(`TauCeti.HeisenbergGroup.nilpotentPart`), so that every element of the Lie algebra is the nilpotent
part of exactly one group element. Since every bracket is central and every iterated bracket
vanishes, the two actions are affine:

`Ad_g A = A + ⁅N, A⁆`, `Ad*_g ν = ν + ⁅N, ν⁆`

(`TauCeti.HeisenbergGroup.adjointRepresentation_apply`,
`TauCeti.HeisenbergGroup.coadjointRepresentation_apply`). In the coordinates `A = a X + b Y + c Z`
and `ν = (p, q, r) = (ν X, ν Y, ν Z)` this reads

`Ad_g (a, b, c) = (a, b, c + x b - y a)`, `Ad*_g (p, q, r) = (p + r y, q - r x, r)`.

Consequently:

* The adjoint orbit of `A` consists of the `B` with the same superdiagonal entries `a, b` and with
  `B₀₂ - A₀₂` in the ideal generated by `a` and `b`
  (`TauCeti.HeisenbergGroup.mem_range_adjointRepresentation_apply_iff`). Over a field `K` it is the
  affine line `a X + b Y + K Z` when `(a, b) ≠ (0, 0)`, and the point `{A}` when `A` is central.
* The coadjoint orbit of `ν` is the affine subspace through `ν` directed by the image of the orbit
  derivative `[ξ] ↦ ⁅ξ, ν⁆` of `Module.Dual.coadjointTangentMap`
  (`TauCeti.HeisenbergGroup.range_coadjointRepresentation_apply`). When `r = ν Z` is a unit it is
  the plane `{μ | μ Z = r}`; when `r = 0` it is the point `{ν}`
  (`TauCeti.HeisenbergGroup.range_coadjointRepresentation_apply_of_isUnit`,
  `TauCeti.HeisenbergGroup.range_coadjointRepresentation_apply_of_eq_zero`). When `r` is a
  non-zero-divisor, the stabilizer of `ν` is the central `z`-axis `{(0, 0, z)}`
  (`TauCeti.HeisenbergGroup.stabilizer_coadjointRepresentation_of_mem_nonZeroDivisors`).

So over a field the nontrivial adjoint orbits are lines, of odd dimension, while the nontrivial
coadjoint orbits are planes, of even dimension, as the symplectic form on a coadjoint orbit
requires. Over any commutative ring, in the coordinates `(p, q)` of the plane `r = const`, the
Kirillov–Kostant–Souriau form `ω_ν([ξ], [η]) = ν ⁅ξ, η⁆` of `Module.Dual.kksForm` satisfies

`r ω_ν(u, v) = dp(u) dq(v) - dq(u) dp(v)`

on the orbit tangent vectors (`Module.Dual.apply_heisenbergZ_mul_kksForm`). So when `r` is a unit
(over a field, when `r ≠ 0`) the form is `ω = dp ∧ dq / r`.

Finally, the trace pairing `⟨M, A⟩ = tr (M A)` of `TauCeti/Algebra/Lie/Heisenberg.lean`
identifies the dual of the Heisenberg Lie algebra with the strictly lower triangular matrices
(`TauCeti.heisenbergDualEquiv`); it only sees the strictly lower triangular part of `M`
(`TauCeti.ker_heisenbergTracePairing`). This pairing is equivariant
(`TauCeti.HeisenbergGroup.coadjointRepresentation_heisenbergTracePairing`), so in this model the
coadjoint action is conjugation followed by projection to the strictly lower triangular part:
`Ad*_g Λ` is the strictly lower triangular part of `g Λ g⁻¹`
(`TauCeti.HeisenbergGroup.coadjointRepresentation_heisenbergDualEquiv_eq_iff`).

## Main definitions

* `TauCeti.HeisenbergGroup.nilpotentPart`: the nilpotent part `g - 1`, in the Lie algebra.
* `TauCeti.HeisenbergGroup.adjointRepresentation`,
  `TauCeti.HeisenbergGroup.coadjointRepresentation`: the adjoint and coadjoint representations.

## References

* A. A. Kirillov, *Lectures on the Orbit Method*, Graduate Studies in Mathematics 64, AMS, 2004,
  Chapter 2 (representations and orbits of the Heisenberg group).
-/

public section

namespace TauCeti

open Matrix LieAlgebra LieModule Module

attribute [local instance 100] LieRing.ofAssociativeRing

variable {R : Type*} [CommRing R]

namespace HeisenbergGroup

/-! ### The nilpotent part -/

/-- The **nilpotent part** `g - 1` of the unipotent matrix `g` of `a`: the strictly upper
triangular matrix with superdiagonal entries `a.x`, `a.y` and corner entry `a.z`, as an element of
the Heisenberg Lie algebra. -/
def nilpotentPart (a : HeisenbergGroup R) : heisenberg R :=
  ⟨toMatrix a - 1, mem_strictUpperTriangular_iff.mpr fun i j hji => by
    fin_cases i <;> fin_cases j <;> simp_all [toMatrix_apply]⟩

@[simp]
theorem coe_nilpotentPart (a : HeisenbergGroup R) :
    (a.nilpotentPart : Matrix (Fin 3) (Fin 3) R) = toMatrix a - 1 := (rfl)

@[simp]
theorem nilpotentPart_apply_zero_one (a : HeisenbergGroup R) :
    (a.nilpotentPart : Matrix (Fin 3) (Fin 3) R) 0 1 = a.x := by
  simp [toMatrix_apply]

@[simp]
theorem nilpotentPart_apply_one_two (a : HeisenbergGroup R) :
    (a.nilpotentPart : Matrix (Fin 3) (Fin 3) R) 1 2 = a.y := by
  simp [toMatrix_apply]

@[simp]
theorem nilpotentPart_apply_zero_two (a : HeisenbergGroup R) :
    (a.nilpotentPart : Matrix (Fin 3) (Fin 3) R) 0 2 = a.z := by
  simp [toMatrix_apply]

/-- **Every element of the Heisenberg Lie algebra is the nilpotent part of exactly one group
element**: `a ↦ g - 1` is a bijection from the Heisenberg group onto its Lie algebra. -/
theorem nilpotentPart_bijective : Function.Bijective (nilpotentPart : HeisenbergGroup R → _) := by
  refine ⟨fun a b h => ?_, fun A => ⟨⟨(A : Matrix (Fin 3) (Fin 3) R) 0 1,
    (A : Matrix (Fin 3) (Fin 3) R) 1 2, (A : Matrix (Fin 3) (Fin 3) R) 0 2⟩, ?_⟩⟩
  · have h01 := congrArg (fun A : heisenberg R => (A : Matrix (Fin 3) (Fin 3) R) 0 1) h
    have h12 := congrArg (fun A : heisenberg R => (A : Matrix (Fin 3) (Fin 3) R) 1 2) h
    have h02 := congrArg (fun A : heisenberg R => (A : Matrix (Fin 3) (Fin 3) R) 0 2) h
    simp only [nilpotentPart_apply_zero_one, nilpotentPart_apply_one_two,
      nilpotentPart_apply_zero_two] at h01 h12 h02
    ext <;> assumption
  · conv_rhs => rw [eq_smul_heisenbergX_add_smul_heisenbergY_add_smul_heisenbergZ A]
    refine Subtype.ext (Matrix.ext fun i j => ?_)
    fin_cases i <;> fin_cases j <;> simp [toMatrix_apply]

/-- **Conjugation by a unipotent matrix is `1 + ad` of its nilpotent part** on the Heisenberg Lie
algebra: `g A g⁻¹ = A + ⁅g - 1, A⁆`, because every product of three strictly upper triangular
`3 × 3` matrices vanishes. -/
theorem toMatrix_mul_mul_toMatrix_inv (a : HeisenbergGroup R) (A : heisenberg R) :
    toMatrix a * (A : Matrix (Fin 3) (Fin 3) R) * toMatrix a⁻¹ =
      (A : Matrix (Fin 3) (Fin 3) R) + (⁅a.nilpotentPart, A⁆ : heisenberg R) := by
  have hA := mem_strictUpperTriangular_iff.mp A.2
  rw [lie_eq_smul_heisenbergZ]
  ext i j
  fin_cases i <;> fin_cases j <;>
    simp [toMatrix_apply, Matrix.mul_apply, Matrix.vecMul, dotProduct, Fin.sum_univ_three, hA]
  ring

/-! ### The adjoint representation -/

variable (R) in
/-- The **adjoint representation** of the Heisenberg group on its Lie algebra, by conjugation
`Ad_g A = g A g⁻¹` of strictly upper triangular matrices by unipotent ones. -/
def adjointRepresentation : Representation R (HeisenbergGroup R) (heisenberg R) where
  toFun a :=
    { toFun A := ⟨toMatrix a * A * toMatrix a⁻¹, by
        rw [toMatrix_mul_mul_toMatrix_inv]
        exact add_mem A.2 (⁅a.nilpotentPart, A⁆ : heisenberg R).2⟩
      map_add' A B := Subtype.ext (by simp [Matrix.mul_add, Matrix.add_mul])
      map_smul' c A := Subtype.ext (by simp) }
  map_one' := LinearMap.ext fun A => Subtype.ext (by simp)
  map_mul' a b := LinearMap.ext fun A => Subtype.ext (by simp [Matrix.mul_assoc])

@[simp]
theorem coe_adjointRepresentation_apply (a : HeisenbergGroup R) (A : heisenberg R) :
    (adjointRepresentation R a A : Matrix (Fin 3) (Fin 3) R) =
      toMatrix a * (A : Matrix (Fin 3) (Fin 3) R) * toMatrix a⁻¹ := (rfl)

/-- **The adjoint action is `1 + ad` of the nilpotent part**: `Ad_g A = A + ⁅g - 1, A⁆`. In
coordinates, `Ad_g (a, b, c) = (a, b, c + x b - y a)`. -/
theorem adjointRepresentation_apply (a : HeisenbergGroup R) (A : heisenberg R) :
    adjointRepresentation R a A = A + ⁅a.nilpotentPart, A⁆ :=
  Subtype.ext (toMatrix_mul_mul_toMatrix_inv a A)

/-- **The adjoint action is by Lie algebra automorphisms.** -/
theorem adjointRepresentation_lie (a : HeisenbergGroup R) (A B : heisenberg R) :
    adjointRepresentation R a ⁅A, B⁆ =
      ⁅adjointRepresentation R a A, adjointRepresentation R a B⁆ := by
  simp [adjointRepresentation_apply, lie_add, add_lie]

/-- **The adjoint orbit of `A` is the set of translates `A + ⁅ξ, A⁆`** of `A` by the image of
`ad A`. -/
theorem range_adjointRepresentation_apply {A : heisenberg R} :
    Set.range (fun a => adjointRepresentation R a A) =
      Set.range fun ξ : heisenberg R => A + ⁅ξ, A⁆ := by
  simp_rw [adjointRepresentation_apply]
  exact nilpotentPart_bijective.2.range_comp fun ξ : heisenberg R => A + ⁅ξ, A⁆

/-- **The adjoint orbits of the Heisenberg group.** An element `B` lies in the adjoint orbit of
`A` exactly when it has the same superdiagonal entries and its corner entry differs from that of
`A` by an element of the ideal generated by the superdiagonal entries of `A`. -/
theorem mem_range_adjointRepresentation_apply_iff {A B : heisenberg R} :
    B ∈ Set.range (fun a => adjointRepresentation R a A) ↔
      (B : Matrix (Fin 3) (Fin 3) R) 0 1 = (A : Matrix (Fin 3) (Fin 3) R) 0 1 ∧
      (B : Matrix (Fin 3) (Fin 3) R) 1 2 = (A : Matrix (Fin 3) (Fin 3) R) 1 2 ∧
      (B : Matrix (Fin 3) (Fin 3) R) 0 2 - (A : Matrix (Fin 3) (Fin 3) R) 0 2 ∈
        Ideal.span {(A : Matrix (Fin 3) (Fin 3) R) 0 1, (A : Matrix (Fin 3) (Fin 3) R) 1 2} := by
  rw [range_adjointRepresentation_apply]
  constructor
  · rintro ⟨ξ, rfl⟩
    refine ⟨by simp [lie_eq_smul_heisenbergZ], by simp [lie_eq_smul_heisenbergZ],
      Ideal.mem_span_pair.mpr ⟨-(ξ : Matrix (Fin 3) (Fin 3) R) 1 2,
        (ξ : Matrix (Fin 3) (Fin 3) R) 0 1, by simp [lie_eq_smul_heisenbergZ]; ring⟩⟩
  · rintro ⟨h01, h12, h02⟩
    obtain ⟨s, t, hst⟩ := Ideal.mem_span_pair.mp h02
    refine ⟨t • heisenbergX R - s • heisenbergY R, ?_⟩
    have hA := mem_strictUpperTriangular_iff.mp A.2
    have hB := mem_strictUpperTriangular_iff.mp B.2
    dsimp only
    rw [lie_eq_smul_heisenbergZ]
    refine Subtype.ext (Matrix.ext fun i j => ?_)
    fin_cases i <;> fin_cases j <;> simp [hA, hB, h01, h12]
    linear_combination hst

/-- **When the superdiagonal entries of `A` generate the unit ideal, the adjoint orbit of `A` is
the affine line `A + R Z`** of all `B` with the same superdiagonal entries. -/
theorem range_adjointRepresentation_apply_of_span_eq_top {A : heisenberg R}
    (hA : Ideal.span {(A : Matrix (Fin 3) (Fin 3) R) 0 1, (A : Matrix (Fin 3) (Fin 3) R) 1 2} = ⊤) :
    Set.range (fun a => adjointRepresentation R a A) =
      {B : heisenberg R | (B : Matrix (Fin 3) (Fin 3) R) 0 1 = (A : Matrix (Fin 3) (Fin 3) R) 0 1 ∧
        (B : Matrix (Fin 3) (Fin 3) R) 1 2 = (A : Matrix (Fin 3) (Fin 3) R) 1 2} := by
  ext B
  rw [mem_range_adjointRepresentation_apply_iff, Set.mem_ofPred_eq]
  simp [hA]

/-- **Over a field, the adjoint orbit of a noncentral element is an affine line**: if
`(A₀₁, A₁₂) ≠ (0, 0)`, the orbit of `A` is `A + K Z`. -/
theorem range_adjointRepresentation_apply_of_ne_zero {K : Type*} [Field K] {A : heisenberg K}
    (hA : (A : Matrix (Fin 3) (Fin 3) K) 0 1 ≠ 0 ∨ (A : Matrix (Fin 3) (Fin 3) K) 1 2 ≠ 0) :
    Set.range (fun a => adjointRepresentation K a A) =
      {B : heisenberg K | (B : Matrix (Fin 3) (Fin 3) K) 0 1 = (A : Matrix (Fin 3) (Fin 3) K) 0 1 ∧
        (B : Matrix (Fin 3) (Fin 3) K) 1 2 = (A : Matrix (Fin 3) (Fin 3) K) 1 2} := by
  refine range_adjointRepresentation_apply_of_span_eq_top ?_
  rcases hA with h | h
  · exact Ideal.eq_top_of_isUnit_mem _ (Ideal.subset_span (by simp)) h.isUnit
  · exact Ideal.eq_top_of_isUnit_mem _ (Ideal.subset_span (by simp)) h.isUnit

/-- **The adjoint orbit of a central element is a point.** -/
theorem range_adjointRepresentation_apply_of_eq_zero {A : heisenberg R}
    (h01 : (A : Matrix (Fin 3) (Fin 3) R) 0 1 = 0) (h12 : (A : Matrix (Fin 3) (Fin 3) R) 1 2 = 0) :
    Set.range (fun a => adjointRepresentation R a A) = {A} := by
  have h : ∀ ξ : heisenberg R, ⁅ξ, A⁆ = 0 := fun ξ => by simp [lie_eq_smul_heisenbergZ, h01, h12]
  simp [range_adjointRepresentation_apply, h]

/-! ### The coadjoint representation -/

variable (R) in
/-- The **coadjoint representation** of the Heisenberg group on the dual of its Lie algebra,
`(Ad*_g ν)(A) = ν (Ad_(g⁻¹) A)`, the dual of the adjoint representation. -/
def coadjointRepresentation : Representation R (HeisenbergGroup R) (Dual R (heisenberg R)) :=
  (adjointRepresentation R).dual

theorem coadjointRepresentation_apply_apply (a : HeisenbergGroup R) (ν : Dual R (heisenberg R))
    (A : heisenberg R) :
    coadjointRepresentation R a ν A = ν (adjointRepresentation R a⁻¹ A) := (rfl)

/-- **The coadjoint action is `1 + ad*` of the nilpotent part**: `Ad*_g ν = ν + ⁅g - 1, ν⁆`, where
`⁅ξ, ν⁆ = -ν ∘ ad ξ` is the infinitesimal coadjoint action. -/
theorem coadjointRepresentation_apply (a : HeisenbergGroup R) (ν : Dual R (heisenberg R)) :
    coadjointRepresentation R a ν = ν + ⁅a.nilpotentPart, ν⁆ := by
  ext A
  rw [coadjointRepresentation_apply_apply, adjointRepresentation_apply]
  simp [lie_eq_smul_heisenbergZ]
  ring

/-- The first coordinate of the coadjoint action: `p ↦ p + r y`. -/
@[simp]
theorem coadjointRepresentation_apply_heisenbergX (a : HeisenbergGroup R)
    (ν : Dual R (heisenberg R)) :
    coadjointRepresentation R a ν (heisenbergX R) =
      ν (heisenbergX R) + a.y * ν (heisenbergZ R) := by
  simp [coadjointRepresentation_apply, lie_eq_smul_heisenbergZ]

/-- The second coordinate of the coadjoint action: `q ↦ q - r x`. -/
@[simp]
theorem coadjointRepresentation_apply_heisenbergY (a : HeisenbergGroup R)
    (ν : Dual R (heisenberg R)) :
    coadjointRepresentation R a ν (heisenbergY R) =
      ν (heisenbergY R) - a.x * ν (heisenbergZ R) := by
  simp [coadjointRepresentation_apply, lie_eq_smul_heisenbergZ, sub_eq_add_neg]

/-- The third coordinate `r = ν Z` of a functional is invariant under the coadjoint action. -/
@[simp]
theorem coadjointRepresentation_apply_heisenbergZ (a : HeisenbergGroup R)
    (ν : Dual R (heisenberg R)) :
    coadjointRepresentation R a ν (heisenbergZ R) = ν (heisenbergZ R) := by
  simp [coadjointRepresentation_apply]

/-- **The coadjoint orbit of `ν` is the affine subspace through `ν` directed by the image of the
orbit derivative** `[ξ] ↦ ⁅ξ, ν⁆` on the algebraic tangent model `𝔥 ⧸ 𝔥_ν`. -/
theorem range_coadjointRepresentation_apply {ν : Dual R (heisenberg R)} :
    Set.range (fun a => coadjointRepresentation R a ν) =
      (ν + ·) '' Set.range ν.coadjointTangentMap := by
  ext μ
  simp only [Set.mem_range, Set.mem_image, coadjointRepresentation_apply]
  constructor
  · rintro ⟨a, rfl⟩
    exact ⟨_, ⟨Submodule.Quotient.mk a.nilpotentPart, rfl⟩, by rw [Dual.coadjointTangentMap_mk]⟩
  · rintro ⟨_, ⟨u, rfl⟩, rfl⟩
    obtain ⟨ξ, rfl⟩ := Submodule.Quotient.mk_surjective _ u
    obtain ⟨a, rfl⟩ := nilpotentPart_bijective.2 ξ
    exact ⟨a, by rw [Dual.coadjointTangentMap_mk]⟩

/-- **The coadjoint orbits of the Heisenberg group.** A functional `μ` lies in the coadjoint orbit
of `ν` exactly when `μ Z = ν Z` and `ν Z` divides both `μ X - ν X` and `μ Y - ν Y`. -/
theorem mem_range_coadjointRepresentation_apply_iff {ν μ : Dual R (heisenberg R)} :
    μ ∈ Set.range (fun a => coadjointRepresentation R a ν) ↔
      μ (heisenbergZ R) = ν (heisenbergZ R) ∧
      ν (heisenbergZ R) ∣ μ (heisenbergX R) - ν (heisenbergX R) ∧
      ν (heisenbergZ R) ∣ μ (heisenbergY R) - ν (heisenbergY R) := by
  constructor
  · rintro ⟨a, rfl⟩
    refine ⟨?_, ⟨a.y, ?_⟩, ⟨-a.x, ?_⟩⟩ <;> simp <;> ring
  · rintro ⟨hZ, ⟨s, hs⟩, ⟨t, ht⟩⟩
    refine ⟨⟨-t, s, 0⟩, (heisenbergBasis R).ext fun k => ?_⟩
    fin_cases k
    · simp only [Fin.zero_eta, heisenbergBasis_zero, coadjointRepresentation_apply_heisenbergX]
      linear_combination -hs
    · simp only [Fin.mk_one, heisenbergBasis_one, coadjointRepresentation_apply_heisenbergY]
      linear_combination -ht
    · simpa using hZ.symm

/-- **The coadjoint orbit through a functional `ν` with `ν Z` a unit is the plane
`{μ | μ Z = ν Z}`.** Over a field this covers every `ν` with `ν Z ≠ 0`. -/
theorem range_coadjointRepresentation_apply_of_isUnit {ν : Dual R (heisenberg R)}
    (hν : IsUnit (ν (heisenbergZ R))) :
    Set.range (fun a => coadjointRepresentation R a ν) =
      {μ | μ (heisenbergZ R) = ν (heisenbergZ R)} := by
  ext μ
  rw [mem_range_coadjointRepresentation_apply_iff, Set.mem_ofPred_eq]
  simp [hν.dvd]

/-- **The coadjoint orbit through a functional vanishing on the centre is a point.** -/
theorem range_coadjointRepresentation_apply_of_eq_zero {ν : Dual R (heisenberg R)}
    (hν : ν (heisenbergZ R) = 0) :
    Set.range (fun a => coadjointRepresentation R a ν) = {ν} := by
  have h : ∀ ξ : heisenberg R, ⁅ξ, ν⁆ = 0 := fun ξ =>
    (mem_lieAnnihilator R (heisenberg R)).mp
      ((Dual.lieAnnihilator_heisenberg_eq_top_iff ν).mpr hν ▸ LieSubalgebra.mem_top ξ)
  simp [coadjointRepresentation_apply, h]

/-- **The coadjoint stabilizer of a functional `ν` with `ν Z` a non-zero-divisor is the central
`z`-axis** `{(0, 0, z)}` of the Heisenberg group. Over a field this covers every `ν` with
`ν Z ≠ 0`. -/
theorem stabilizer_coadjointRepresentation_of_mem_nonZeroDivisors {ν : Dual R (heisenberg R)}
    (hν : ν (heisenbergZ R) ∈ nonZeroDivisors R) :
    (coadjointRepresentation R).stabilizer ν = zAxis := by
  ext a
  rw [Representation.mem_stabilizer, mem_zAxis_iff]
  constructor
  · intro h
    have hx := LinearMap.congr_fun h (heisenbergX R)
    have hy := LinearMap.congr_fun h (heisenbergY R)
    simp only [coadjointRepresentation_apply_heisenbergX, add_eq_left,
      coadjointRepresentation_apply_heisenbergY, sub_eq_self] at hx hy
    exact ⟨(mul_right_mem_nonZeroDivisors_eq_zero_iff hν).mp hy,
      (mul_right_mem_nonZeroDivisors_eq_zero_iff hν).mp hx⟩
  · rintro ⟨hx, hy⟩
    refine (heisenbergBasis R).ext fun k => ?_
    fin_cases k <;> simp [hx, hy]

/-- **A functional vanishing on the centre is fixed by the whole Heisenberg group.** -/
theorem stabilizer_coadjointRepresentation_of_eq_zero {ν : Dual R (heisenberg R)}
    (hν : ν (heisenbergZ R) = 0) :
    (coadjointRepresentation R).stabilizer ν = ⊤ := by
  refine eq_top_iff.mpr fun a _ => Representation.mem_stabilizer.mpr ?_
  have h := range_coadjointRepresentation_apply_of_eq_zero hν
  exact Set.mem_singleton_iff.mp (h ▸ Set.mem_range_self a)

end HeisenbergGroup

/-! ### The Kirillov–Kostant–Souriau form of a coadjoint orbit -/

/-- **The orbit tangent vectors have vanishing third coordinate**: the image `⁅ξ, ν⁆` of the orbit
derivative is tangent to the plane `r = ν Z`. -/
@[simp]
theorem _root_.Module.Dual.coadjointTangentMap_apply_heisenbergZ (ν : Dual R (heisenberg R))
    (u : heisenberg R ⧸ (lieAnnihilator R (heisenberg R) ν).toSubmodule) :
    ν.coadjointTangentMap u (heisenbergZ R) = 0 := by
  induction u using Submodule.Quotient.induction_on with
  | H ξ => simp

/-- **The orbit derivative of a coadjoint orbit through `ν` with `ν Z` a unit maps the tangent
model onto the plane `{μ | μ Z = 0}`**, the tangent plane of the orbit `{μ | μ Z = ν Z}`. -/
theorem _root_.Module.Dual.range_coadjointTangentMap_heisenberg_of_isUnit
    {ν : Dual R (heisenberg R)} (hν : IsUnit (ν (heisenbergZ R))) :
    LinearMap.range ν.coadjointTangentMap =
      LinearMap.ker (Dual.eval R (heisenberg R) (heisenbergZ R)) := by
  refine le_antisymm (fun μ ⟨u, hu⟩ => hu ▸ ν.coadjointTangentMap_apply_heisenbergZ u)
    fun μ hμ => ?_
  rw [LinearMap.mem_ker, Dual.eval_apply] at hμ
  obtain ⟨r, hr⟩ := hν.exists_left_inv
  refine ⟨Submodule.Quotient.mk
    ((-(r * μ (heisenbergY R))) • heisenbergX R + (r * μ (heisenbergX R)) • heisenbergY R), ?_⟩
  refine (heisenbergBasis R).ext fun k => ?_
  fin_cases k <;> simp [Dual.coadjointTangentMap_mk, lie_eq_smul_heisenbergZ, hμ]
  · linear_combination μ (heisenbergX R) * hr
  · linear_combination μ (heisenbergY R) * hr

/-- **`r` times the Kirillov–Kostant–Souriau form of a Heisenberg coadjoint orbit is `dp ∧ dq`.**
For tangent vectors `u`, `v` of the algebraic tangent model at `ν`, read in the coordinates
`p = μ X` and `q = μ Y` of their images `μ` under the orbit derivative, the form
`ω_ν(u, v)` times `r = ν Z` is the area form `dp ∧ dq`, over any commutative ring. When `r` is a
unit (over a field, when `r ≠ 0`), the form is therefore `dp ∧ dq / r`. -/
theorem _root_.Module.Dual.apply_heisenbergZ_mul_kksForm (ν : Dual R (heisenberg R))
    (u v : heisenberg R ⧸ (lieAnnihilator R (heisenberg R) ν).toSubmodule) :
    ν (heisenbergZ R) * ν.kksForm u v =
      ν.coadjointTangentMap u (heisenbergX R) * ν.coadjointTangentMap v (heisenbergY R) -
        ν.coadjointTangentMap u (heisenbergY R) * ν.coadjointTangentMap v (heisenbergX R) := by
  induction u using Submodule.Quotient.induction_on with
  | H ξ =>
  induction v using Submodule.Quotient.induction_on with
  | H η =>
  simp [Dual.kksForm_mk, Dual.coadjointTangentMap_mk, lie_eq_smul_heisenbergZ]
  ring

namespace HeisenbergGroup

/-- **The trace pairing is equivariant**: the coadjoint action on the functional `tr (M ·)` is
conjugation of `M`, `Ad*_g ⟨M, ·⟩ = ⟨g M g⁻¹, ·⟩`. -/
theorem coadjointRepresentation_heisenbergTracePairing (a : HeisenbergGroup R)
    (M : Matrix (Fin 3) (Fin 3) R) :
    coadjointRepresentation R a (heisenbergTracePairing R M) =
      heisenbergTracePairing R (toMatrix a * M * toMatrix a⁻¹) := by
  ext A
  rw [coadjointRepresentation_apply_apply, heisenbergTracePairing_apply,
    heisenbergTracePairing_apply, coe_adjointRepresentation_apply, inv_inv,
    ← Matrix.mul_assoc, ← Matrix.mul_assoc, Matrix.trace_mul_comm, ← Matrix.mul_assoc,
    ← Matrix.mul_assoc]

/-- **The coadjoint action in the lower triangular model**: `Ad*_g` sends the functional of a
strictly lower triangular `Λ` to that of `Λ'` exactly when `Λ'` is the strictly lower triangular
part of `g Λ g⁻¹`, that is, when `g Λ g⁻¹ - Λ'` is upper triangular. -/
theorem coadjointRepresentation_heisenbergDualEquiv_eq_iff (a : HeisenbergGroup R)
    (Λ Λ' : strictLowerTriangular R (Fin 3)) :
    coadjointRepresentation R a (heisenbergDualEquiv R Λ) = heisenbergDualEquiv R Λ' ↔
      toMatrix a * (Λ : Matrix (Fin 3) (Fin 3) R) * toMatrix a⁻¹ - Λ' ∈
        upperTriangular R (Fin 3) := by
  rw [heisenbergDualEquiv_apply, heisenbergDualEquiv_apply,
    coadjointRepresentation_heisenbergTracePairing, ← sub_eq_zero, ← map_sub,
    ← LinearMap.mem_ker, ker_heisenbergTracePairing, LieSubalgebra.mem_toSubmodule]

end HeisenbergGroup

